  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "86wTGkVkpKj0uKRVDejpVjjVDqE=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:34:56.000Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Powerhouse offers three ways to project the operation log into something queryable, and the Cookbook's Analytics Processor recipe gives the selection criterion as a table rather than a preference:\n\n| | analytics-processor | custom-read-model | relational-db-subgraph |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Storage model | Dimensional time-series (source, metric, dimensions, unit) | In-memory map | Relational rows (Kysely schema) |\n| Query style | Time-bucketed aggregates, dimension rollups via `lod` | Direct lookup | Arbitrary SQL: joins, filters, ordering |\n| Best for | \"Total per category per month in USD\" \u2014 dashboards, budgets, spend analytics | Simple derived counts | Searchable catalogs, tag filters |\n\nThe decisive test is stated compactly: use the analytics engine when queries are shaped like *aggregate metric X, grouped by dimension Y, over period Z, rolled up to depth N*. Use a relational DB when you need relational queries. Use a custom read model for lightweight synchronous counters.\n\nThe dimensions that actually separate them are query shape and where the result lives, not data volume. A custom `IReadModel` is in-memory and synchronous, which is why it suits counters and suits nothing that must survive a restart on its own. The analytics engine is the only one of the three that understands *time intervals* natively \u2014 values carry a start and end, deltas are first class, and rollups happen at query time via `lod` \u2014 so re-bucketing \"monthly\" into \"quarterly\" needs no reprojection. A relational read model is the only one that can answer a question you did not anticipate when you designed the projection.\n\nThe analytics recipe also surfaces two modelling constraints that follow from picking it: updates and deletes must be written as **compensating entries** (\u2212old, +new) rather than mutations, and values should be dated by *business time* (when the money was spent) rather than event or processing time, so a March correction to a January expense still lands in January.\n\nProvenance: academy `05-Lookup/00-Cookbook.md`, Analytics Processor recipe (\"When to use the analytics engine vs a SQL read model\"), academy 6.2.2-dev.45.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T12:34:56.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:34:56.000Z"
    }
  },
